WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:06.060
Hello, if you want to know what LoRA is and how&nbsp;
it helps us to finetune huge language models&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:12.660
such as GPT3 or LLaMA, then this video is right&nbsp;
for you! LoRA is short for Low-Rank Adaptation&nbsp;

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:17.760
and comes in really handy, because: The cool&nbsp;
chatbots and language models that we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:25.200
been using, such as ChatGPT, LLaMA 2, Claude,&nbsp;
Falcon, and so on, are very general purpose.&nbsp;

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.740
Prompting them correctly by&nbsp;
asking the question right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:33.360
plus showing them some examples&nbsp;
in the input only gets us so far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:37.440
and usually works for domains that the&nbsp;
model has already seen in pretraining.&nbsp;

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:41.340
To really specialize them for a&nbsp;
task or to a different domain to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:46.620
make them become a banking chatbot and get&nbsp;
financial skills, or a medical chatbot,&nbsp;

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:52.320
we want to finetune them on our&nbsp;
smaller dataset, such as, idk,&nbsp;

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:58.440
transcripts of videos from this channel if we want&nbsp;
the LLaMA model to impersonate Ms. Coffee Bean.&nbsp;

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:02.940
But when finetuning
large language models – at least the open source&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:09.780
ones which we can download – we quickly run into
a problem with their size: Highly capable Large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:15.180
Language Models are huge because they contain&nbsp;
tens to hundreds of billions of parameters.&nbsp;

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:21.900
And during finetuning, we need to load all these&nbsp;
parameters into our GPU (just like for inference),&nbsp;

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:25.860
but we also need roughly&nbsp;
double the amount of space&nbsp;

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:30.720
to save the respective gradient for&nbsp;
each parameter. Just as a reminder of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:35.100
basic neural network training: we need&nbsp;
the gradients during backpropagation to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:40.620
adapt the weights according to the loss we&nbsp;
computed on instances from the new task.&nbsp;

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:46.620
[How LoRA works] But with LoRA, we can finetune&nbsp;
large models with only a fraction of the cost.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:52.500
LoRA was introduced by this paper from Microsoft&nbsp;
and is surprisingly less complicated than expected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:58.320
and we’ll explain it. It works like this:
With LoRA, instead of modifying all these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:02.820
model weights during finetuning,
we freeze them, meaning that we leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:06.720
them unchanged and do not do anything&nbsp;
to the copy of the downloaded model.&nbsp;

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:12.720
Instead, we add a separate set of weights.&nbsp;
These finetuned weights after an ideal LoRA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:17.940
training process, represent the differences&nbsp;
we need to add to the pretrained parameters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:22.980
to make the model perfect for solving the&nbsp;
finetuning task. But come on, what did we do?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:28.500
We now have doubled the number of parameters&nbsp;
on our hard drive. Yes, but it is double the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:34.800
amount on our hard drive and not on our GPU.
On our GPU, when we load the finetuned model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:38.760
for inference, we add the fine-tuned&nbsp;
weights to the pre-trained weights,&nbsp;

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:43.320
so we still have one set of weights for&nbsp;
inference in our video memory, not two.&nbsp;

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:49.200
The finetuned weights can be seen as modifications&nbsp;
to the original weights which we can merge into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:54.240
the model if needed. Okay, so during inference&nbsp;
we did not increase the need of memory for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:59.820
our GPU, but how do we finetune the network
and adjust these additional difference parameters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:04.860
and how can this be more efficient, since their&nbsp;
number is the same as the original number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:12.300
parameters? Well, this is the trick in LoRA: it&nbsp;
reduces the number of trainable parameters. How?&nbsp;

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:16.920
Well, you maybe know
that a layer in a neural network is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:22.560
matrix multiplication to the input of the layer&nbsp;
plus the addition of a so-called bias vector,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:27.900
followed by a nonlinear operation. The weights of&nbsp;
the network are the entries of the matrix, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:33.240
are the numbers we need to adjust in training or&nbsp;
finetuning. The larger the number of parameters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:41.160
the larger the matrices, so GPT-3 with 175 billion&nbsp;
parameters has much larger matrices than GPT-2 of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:46.320
“only” 1.5 billion parameters.
Now, every matrix has a rank,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:53.940
which is a number counting how many linearly&nbsp;
independent columns the matrix has. Linearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.940 --> 00:04:01.440
dependent means that we can get that column by&nbsp;
combining other columns from the matrix. If we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:08.340
remove linearly dependent columns from the matrix,&nbsp;
we reduce the matrix dimension, but without losing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:13.080
information – because that information was&nbsp;
already there since we can get that linearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:19.500
dependent column from other columns in the matrix.
So LoRA’s idea is that we do not need to optimize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:25.440
the full rank matrices that have high dimensions,&nbsp;
thus a lot of numbers, but rather we should do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:32.580
a low rank decomposition, by representing these&nbsp;
weights as the multiplication of two matrices A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:38.340
and B. We gain computational efficiency, because&nbsp;
A and B together contain less numbers than the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:44.220
original weight matrix – in this example we have&nbsp;
6 numbers instead of 9 – thus these are less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:49.080
numbers we need to tune. And the bigger the&nbsp;
original weight matrices, the higher the gains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:55.920
from doing this decomposition to a low rank r.&nbsp;
The effect of writing delta W as a multiplication&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:01.500
of two smaller matrices A and B is that, we reduce&nbsp;
the dimensionality of the weight matrix through A,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:08.040
thus we remove linearly dependent columns, and&nbsp;
we regain the original dimensionality through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:13.980
B again. The hyperparameter we need to choose&nbsp;
is the rank r, because we do not know what the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:19.980
intrinsic rank of the weight matrix is and we&nbsp;
implicitly remove hopefully linearly dependent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:25.980
columns by the AB decomposition: if we choose&nbsp;
the rank too low, we reduce the dimensionality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:31.440
too much and lose information, because we&nbsp;
implicitly deleted linearly independent columns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:37.800
If we choose it too high, we keep too many&nbsp;
parameters that are linearly dependent and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:44.820
waste computation. We initialize A from a Gaussian&nbsp;
distribution and B with 0 and let backpropagation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:50.880
figure out what the right numbers in the matrices&nbsp;
A and B are according to our finetuning objective.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:56.400
So LoRA in a nutshell means that instead&nbsp;
of tuning the large weight matrix delta W,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:01.500
we tune the smaller matrices A and B.&nbsp;
Told you that it is not super complicated!&nbsp;

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:05.700
So after we’ve found the finetuned&nbsp;
weights by optimizing A and B,&nbsp;

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:08.580
we should not forget to add&nbsp;
them to the original model&nbsp;

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:12.660
to make inference with the LoRA&nbsp;
finetuned model. And we’re done.&nbsp;

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:17.760
But why choose LoRA and not other&nbsp;
approaches that adapt models in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:22.200
parameter- and compute efficient way?
One common alternative to LoRA&nbsp;

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:26.760
is adding so-called Adapter Layers
in each Transformer block. During fine-tuning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:33.000
we only adapt the parameters of these layers and&nbsp;
leave the rest unchanged. Since these adapter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:38.220
layers have very few parameters, even as few&nbsp;
as 1% of the transformer’s Feed forward layers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:43.680
it means that during training, it is very compute&nbsp;
efficient. But the problem is that large networks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:48.000
are usually parallelized on hardware,
while adapter layers must be processed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:52.980
sequentially. This means that during inference,&nbsp;
adapters introduce a noticeable latency.&nbsp;

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:58.080
Another common approach is prefix tuning&nbsp;
which is a continuous and automated version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:03.180
of prompt engineering. Instead of manually&nbsp;
choosing the right words for the input to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:08.940
prompt the model to give the right answer,
prefix tuning bypasses the word representation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:14.940
step: We add input vectors that do not stand for&nbsp;
any words in particular, they are just vectors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:21.060
that we initialize randomly and are called&nbsp;
prefix. We tune the entries of the vectors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:26.460
with backpropagation until the model delivers the&nbsp;
correct answer. Compared to Prompt Engineering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:32.100
where the vectors represent words, in prefix&nbsp;
tuning they do not. The problem with prefix tuning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:39.180
is that it occupies part of the sequence length&nbsp;
and reduces the size of the effective input. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:43.140
prefix tuning is difficult to optimize&nbsp;
and the number of trainable parameters is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:48.000
hard to choose: if you delete parameters,&nbsp;
the performance can unexpectedly improve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:54.600
but also vice-versa, there is not a clear law.
So sure, Adapters or Prefix tuning are still very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:59.760
popular, but since LoRA came out it has been only&nbsp;
increasing in popularity over the alternatives.&nbsp;

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:05.820
This was our little introduction to LoRA. Do not&nbsp;
forget to hit the like button if LoRA is clearer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:11.880
to you now after watching this video. We hope&nbsp;
to see you next time, so subscribe! Okay, bye!

